Outside Hand\" requires terminals or honors in every meld and pair. Your melds are all middle Dots (B5-B9), none are terminals or honors, so Outside Hand is impossible.  \n3. **Prevalent/Seat Winds**: Prevailing Wind is West (F3), Seat Wind is South (F2). F4 is North Wind, not matching, so no extra points.  \n4. **Safety**: Discarding a tile that might be dangerous for other players. From history, many tiles have been played. W5 has not been played recently; F4 was played by you earlier (Play F4 in your history), and other F4s might be out. Both seem relatively safe.  \n5. **Hand completion**: Whichever you keep, you need to draw its duplicate. Probability wise, W5 has 4 left vs F4 3 left, slightly better odds.  \n6. **Pattern possibilities**: With your melds, you already have \"All Chows\" (2 points) because all four melds are Chows? Wait, one meld is a Pung (B5). So not all Chows. You have \"No Honors\" (1 point) if no honors in hand, but keeping F4 would break that. You have \"All Simples\" (2 points) if no terminals or honors; B5-B9 are simples, W5 is simple, F4 is honor, so keeping W5 preserves All Simples.  \nCurrent patterns: Your melds are three Chows and one Pung, all in Dots, all simples. If you win with W5 pair, you could have: All Simples (2), maybe Pure Straight? No, because your Chows are B6-B7-B8 (twice) and B7-B8-B9, not a full 1-9 sequence. Possibly Short Straight (1) for two successive Chows? But they are exposed, and Short Straight is for chows in hand? Actually, patterns count regardless of exposure. You have two identical Chows (B6-B7-B8 twice), which is \"Pure Double Chow\" (1 point). Also, you have a Pung of simples, no extra. So likely minimal points. To reach 8 points minimum, you need more. With W5 pair, possible add: Self-Drawn (1) if you draw it, Single Wait (1) if waiting for one tile. That's still low. But wait, you have four melds already, so you just need a pair. The winning hand will be four melds + pair. You must assess if it meets 